@*
    The switch between the main store and the workshop's spares.

    It sits in the toolbar of every screen that reads stock, and it is the one
    thing standing between "two books" and "one confusing list": whatever is on
    screen belongs to the book named here, and nothing else.

    Deliberately does no data access of its own. A child component's
    OnInitializedAsync runs while the parent page's is still awaiting, and both
    share one scoped DbContext - querying here raised "a second operation was
    started on this context" and took the whole page to a 500. The page loads
    the books as part of its own sequential load and hands them down.

    Hidden entirely when only one book exists, so an install that never split
    its stock is not asked to choose between one option.
*@

@if (Books.Count > 1)
{
    <MudSelect T="int" Value="Selected" ValueChanged="OnChanged" Dense="true"
               Margin="Margin.Dense" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Class="mt-0"
               Style="max-width:230px"
               Adornment="Adornment.Start"
               AdornmentIcon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.LibraryBooks">
        @foreach (var book in Books)
        {
            <MudSelectItem T="int" Value="@book.Id">@book.Name</MudSelectItem>
        }
    </MudSelect>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The books to choose between, loaded by the host page.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<StockDomain> Books { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The book currently on screen.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Selected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the newly chosen book, for the page to act on.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<int> SelectedChanged { get; set; }

    private Task OnChanged(int value) => SelectedChanged.InvokeAsync(value);
}
